Hi Rinika, I’ve completed the linked application analysis and tested the proposed fix.

The issue is caused by the current ROW_NUMBER() logic partitioning by LINKED_APPLICATION_NUMBER and only ordering by EFF_START_DATE DESC. Where multiple application IDs have the same effective start date, the record selected as RN=1 can be arbitrary.

I tested a deterministic rule:

* prioritise COMPLETE where it exists;
* otherwise prioritise a non-WITHDRAWN application;
* if all applications are WITHDRAWN, select the latest application;
* use EFF_START_DATE DESC, APPLICATION_IDENTIFIER DESC as the deterministic ordering.

Validation results were all clean:

* groups containing COMPLETE but RN=1 not COMPLETE: 0
* all-WITHDRAWN groups where RN=1 was not the latest application: 0
* mixed groups containing a non-WITHDRAWN application but RN=1 selected WITHDRAWN: 0

So this logic removes the arbitrary record selection and gives us a deterministic application to retain for each linked application group.